00:01:53.058 --> 00:01:59.958
TC Energy put up a 26-year dividend increased streak and said 98% of earnings sat in regulated

00:01:59.958 --> 00:02:05.938
assets or long-term take-or-pay contracts last quarter, backing an 88% quarterly payout

00:02:05.938 --> 00:02:08.038
and a 3.8% forward yield.

00:02:08.578 --> 00:02:14.318
It placed $2.5 billion of projects into service in the first nine months, while daily average

00:02:14.318 --> 00:02:21.438
flows rose 6% on Canadian pipelines, 3% on U.S. pipelines, and 8% on Mexican pipelines.

00:02:21.438 --> 00:02:30.018
Ajusted net income reached $1.1 billion, or $0.94 per share, up 22.1% from the year-ago quarter,

00:02:30.518 --> 00:02:35.858
and adjusted earnings before interest taxes depreciation and amortization, or EBITDA,

00:02:36.398 --> 00:02:38.758
climbed $11.1% to $3 billion.

00:02:39.578 --> 00:02:44.498
98% contracted earnings work like a winter-heating contract you lock in October.

00:02:44.998 --> 00:02:49.738
The rate is known, the truck still shows up, and a cold snap does not rewrite the bill

00:02:49.738 --> 00:02:54.478
the way a leader bought on the spot would. Open your brokerage screener, set the exchange

00:02:54.478 --> 00:03:00.098
to TSX, restrict to energy infrastructure names, require at least 20 years of dividend

00:03:00.098 --> 00:03:05.978
growth and adjusted EBITDA growth above 10% year over year, then open the latest quarterly

00:03:05.978 --> 00:03:10.878
report and read the percentage of earnings from contracted sources. That filter can

00:03:10.878 --> 00:03:15.958
fail when contract renewals price in at lower rates or a large project overruns and coverage

00:03:15.958 --> 00:03:21.558
ratio's compress, which is what infrastructure names saw during 2022 rate hike volatility.

00:03:22.198 --> 00:03:26.518
You see a TSX pair with a long increased streak and a 4% looking yield.

00:03:27.258 --> 00:03:31.338
